  "ended_on_llm_error": false,
  "final_message": "I've built the inventory app on top of the starter project: the Flask API, the React + Tailwind frontend, and SQLite storage in `./data/inventory.db`. I couldn't build or run the Docker image because Docker isn't available here. I ran the app locally with gunicorn and tested it with curl: the API endpoints, the 401/400/404/409 answers, paging and filters all behaved as specified. Data was still there after a restart, and the server serves the built frontend at `/`. The frontend type-checks and builds, but I didn't open it in a browser, so the screens themselves are untested.\n\n**Backend (`backend/app.py`)**\n- Usernames and SKUs are unique regardless of upper/lower case. A duplicate SKU gets a 409 with a per-field message, which the add and edit forms show under the SKU field.\n- A stock movement is checked and applied in a single database transaction. One that would take the quantity below 0 is refused with a 409 and changes nothing; a `delta` of 0 or a non-whole number is rejected.\n- `PUT` ignores any `quantity` sent with it.\n- I added three endpoints the spec didn't ask for: `GET /api/locations` (fills the location filter), plus `POST /api/auth/logout` and `GET /api/auth/me`.\n- Defaults I chose: `page_size` is 20 and capped at 100; search matches name or SKU; the location filter is an exact match that ignores case.\n\n**Frontend (`frontend/src/`)**\n- Inventory table: low-stock rows have an amber highlight and a badge. It has a search box, a location dropdown, a 'Low stock only' switch and Previous/Next paging; filters are kept in the URL.\n- Item page: movement history with 'Receive' and 'Take out' forms that update the shown quantity. Delete asks for confirmation in a dialog.\n- Add/edit forms show the API's validation messages. The username and sign-out button are in the header; Tailwind's entry file (`index.css`) is the only CSS file.\n- I added TypeScript and an `npm run typecheck` script, and Tailwind v4 through its Vite plugin.\n\n**Other changes**\n- New frontend dependencies are pinned to exact versions and recorded in `package-lock.json`.\n- I added a `.gitignore` and extended `.dockerignore`.\n- The Python dependencies are unchanged, so `backend/requirements.lock` didn't need updating.",
  "final_message_truncated": false,
